1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,720
Gate is ops-ready and still structurally
muddy: controllers call repositories,
tariff rules sit in a @RestController, JPA
entities serialize straight to truckers.

2
00:00:09,839 --> 00:00:18,839
Layered architecture is the classic Spring
map — web → service → persistence — with
dependency arrows pointing inward toward
the domain, not outward toward frameworks.

3
00:00:18,960 --> 00:00:26,832
In a harbor app the packages usually look
like: Look at this text for a moment. Look
at this java for a moment.

4
00:00:26,952 --> 00:00:38,736
Discipline at the boundary: HTTP DTOs in
web, domain commands in service/domain,
JPA entities stay in persistence unless
you consciously accept the coupling. Walk
a leak.

5
00:00:38,856 --> 00:00:48,695
GateRelease entity gains a Lazy
collection; Jackson serializes it on the
way out; a trucker GET triggers
LazyInitializationException or an
accidental N+1.

6
00:00:48,816 --> 00:00:55,752
The layered fix is a dedicated
CheckInResponse assembled in the service —
not “open session in view” as
architecture.

7
00:00:55,871 --> 00:01:05,135
Another leak: controller injects
GateLedgerRepository and duplicates
release rules for a “quick” admin path
until two definitions of release diverge
at 2am.

8
00:01:05,256 --> 00:01:13,487
ArchUnit can enforce “web does not import
persistence” when code review fatigue sets
in. Look at this java for a moment.

9
00:01:13,608 --> 00:01:23,975
Runtime and team symptoms of layer
collapse: every feature touches four
packages for one field rename; mobile
breaks when a JPA column rename ships
because the entity was the API;

10
00:01:24,096 --> 00:01:30,024
transactional boundaries unclear because
repositories are called from controllers
without a service.

11
00:01:30,144 --> 00:01:39,624
Layers still help onboarding — most Spring
developers can navigate
web/service/persistence in minutes — but
package names without dependency direction
are cosplay.

12
00:01:39,743 --> 00:01:52,079
Trade-offs: strict layering adds mapping
boilerplate (DTO ↔ domain ↔ entity). That
cost buys independent change rates: HTTP
can version while persistence evolves.

13
00:01:52,200 --> 00:01:58,775
For tiny admin CRUD, a thinner path may be
fine — document the exception so gate
release does not copy it.

14
00:01:58,896 --> 00:02:09,264
A single service package that absorbs
everything becomes a dumping ground; split
by capability (release, tariff, ais)
before you invent microservices to escape
the ball of mud.

15
00:02:09,384 --> 00:02:19,032
Onboarding test: a new engineer should
find “where does check-in authorization
live?” in one service method, not half in
the controller and half in a repository
@Query.

16
00:02:19,152 --> 00:02:26,304
When that answer takes a tour of four
packages with duplicated ifs, layers have
already collapsed regardless of folder
names.

17
00:02:26,424 --> 00:02:34,824
Fix by moving policy inward and leaving
web as translation — status codes, JSON,
validation annotations — not berth or
tariff rules.

18
00:02:34,944 --> 00:02:46,584
A misconception is renaming packages
without changing dependencies and calling
it architecture. Another is a single
service package that becomes a dumping
ground for everything not named
controller.

19
00:02:46,704 --> 00:02:53,448
A third is forbidding DTOs “to move
faster” and then breaking mobile clients
when a JPA field rename ships.

20
00:02:53,568 --> 00:03:01,176
When the pain is “domain must not know
Spring Web or JPA at all,” layers need
ports, adapters, and an inside that stays
pure.

21
00:03:01,296 --> 00:03:10,608
That tightening is hexagonal architecture
around gate release — same harbor check-in
story, stricter arrows, fewer excuses for
a controller that speaks SQL.
